\paragraph{LLM-specific challenges.} Applying MH-DIF to LLMs introduces assumptions absent in educational testing. First, models trained on overlapping corpora violate local independence; permutation simulation (1,000 iterations $\times$ 8 subjects, Q3 $= 4.6$--$34.2\%$) confirms LD-attributable FPR inflation $< 0.001$ pp under cross-subject matching with ETS~C (\Cref{app:method_details}). Second, fine-tuned variants and merges within model families are not independent examinees; graduated de-duplication shows family structure does not inflate C\% (\Cref{sec:structural}). Third, the ability distribution is non-normal (bimodal from base vs.\ instruction-tuned models), motivating quintile-based rather than parametric stratification. Fourth, MMLU's 57 subjects span multiple latent dimensions, while MH assumes unidimensionality; cross-subject matching and within-subject DIF checks address this (\Cref{sec:structural}). MH-DIF is non-parametric and does not assume a specific IRT model. Unlike standard purified MH that conditions on total test score, we match on cross-subject total score to avoid criterion contamination when target-subject items themselves may be compromised---an adaptation specific to multi-domain LLM benchmarks where subjects may be differentially affected.

\subsection{Cohort Designs}
\label{sec:cohorts}

We construct five cohort comparisons: (1)~\emph{Temporal}: 2023 ($N = 1{,}080$) vs.\ 2024 ($N = 807$) models, the primary comparison; (2)~\emph{Ability-quintile}: Q1--Q2 vs.\ Q4--Q5 within the same temporal window; (3)~\emph{Within-family}: Llama-2 ($N = 161$) vs.\ Llama-3 ($N = 308$), isolating generational changes within a single family; (4)~\emph{Within-subject}: per-subject DIF to test domain concentration; and (5)~\emph{Random split}: two random halves of the 2023 cohort ($N = 540$ each) as a sanity check.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Injection Framework}
\label{sec:injection}

To evaluate whether MH-DIF can detect differential contamination when it is present, we construct a semi-synthetic framework with controlled ground truth.

\paragraph{Procedure.} From items classified as ETS~A in the uninjected data (negligible DIF), we select 20\% uniformly at random as the ``contaminated'' set. For each contaminated item, we flip incorrect responses to correct in the focal group at exploitation rate $p \in \{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\}$. We then run MH-DIF on the modified response matrix and measure detection performance. We report $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = \mathrm{TPR}(\text{injected}) - \mathrm{TPR}(\text{baseline})$ and $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = \mathrm{FPR}(\text{injected}) - \mathrm{FPR}(\text{baseline})$, where TPR is the true positive rate on injected items and FPR is the false positive rate on uninjected items. Each injection level is repeated across 5 random seeds for uncertainty estimation. Because binary response flipping represents a worst-case uniform injection pattern, reported $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ values constitute upper-bound estimates; realistic contamination (partial memorization, format-specific advantages) would produce weaker signals.

\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.} The choice of matching variable is critical for valid semi-synthetic evaluation. When contamination is injected into items within subject $X$, the total score on subject $X$ absorbs part of the injection signal. Using this contaminated total score as the MH matching variable violates the conditional independence assumption and inflates FPR. Cross-subject matching resolves this by computing the matching variable from all subjects \emph{other than} the one being tested.

\paragraph{Remark (Zero FPR under single-subject matching).}
\label{prop:fpr}
Let $M_i^{(-s)} = \sum_{j \notin s} Y_{ij}$ be the matching variable for examinee $i$ on focal subject $s$. Under single-subject injection (contamination confined to subject $s$), the tested item belongs to $s$ and does not contribute to $M_i^{(-s)}$; injecting contamination into $s$ leaves $M_i^{(-s)}$ unchanged. Therefore $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0$ by construction.

When contamination spans multiple subjects simultaneously, $M_i^{(-s)}$ may absorb signal from other contaminated subjects, potentially inflating FPR. Empirically, this guarantee extends to multi-domain contamination affecting $\leq$9\% of subjects ($k \leq 5$), with graceful degradation beyond this boundary (\Cref{sec:semisynthetic}).

Empirically, $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$ across all five MMLU domains (bootstrap 95\% CI $= [0.000, 0.000]$). $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0$ is a constructive guarantee of the matching strategy, independent of injection realism, whereas $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ constitutes an upper-bound estimate. We adopt cross-subject matching as the standard protocol.

\end{figure}

We apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), to our knowledge the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks, complementing concurrent work on anchor-item calibration~\citep{habba2026growingpains} and addressing the fragility of existing detectors under post-training perturbation~\citep{wang2026fragility}. Two structural controls, a sensitivity check, and replication across all six METABENCH benchmarks confirm that the $\sim$31\% temporal DIF rate is not a methodological artifact (\Cref{sec:temporal,sec:structural,sec:gsm8k}).

DIF should be understood as a diagnostic lens rather than a quality filter: DIF-flagged items concentrate discriminative signal ($z = -8$ to $-49$ vs.\ random removal; \Cref{sec:downstream}), and model families show distinctive DIF profiles.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\enlargethispage*{8\baselineskip}

\subsection{Implications for Benchmark Temporal Comparability}
\label{sec:disc_temporal}

\looseness=-1
Temporal DIF decomposes benchmark differences into impact (ability differences removed by matching, $\sim$2.4~pp), DIF (item-level differences after ability control, $\sim$28--29\% floor, predominantly item-specific), and attribution (underdetermined without training data). In standardized educational testing, ETS~C rates typically range from 5--15\%~\citep{clauser1998dif}; our 31\% rate is 2--6$\times$ higher.

\looseness=-1
Temporal DIF detects \emph{that} items function differently, not \emph{why}. Three non-mutually-exclusive mechanisms may drive the signal: (1)~differential contamination, where asymmetric training data exposure changes item difficulty for one cohort; (2)~capability evolution, where genuine skill acquisition alters the relationship between ability and item response; (3)~training methodology shifts, where RLHF, chain-of-thought prompting, or other post-training techniques change response patterns independent of raw ability. Distinguishing these requires access to training data, which is unavailable for most models.

\looseness=-1
Our diagnostic approach complements benchmark reform efforts such as MMLU-CF~\citep{zhao2025mmlucf}, which reconstructs contamination-free items, and dynamic benchmarks that generate novel items per evaluation cycle. DIF monitoring provides a low-cost alternative when reconstruction is infeasible: rather than replacing items, it identifies which existing items remain measurement-invariant.

\looseness=-1
MMLU contains documented ground truth errors~\citep{gema2024mmlu}, which could contribute to DIF if newer models learn correct answers that differ from the keyed response. [Overlap analysis results pending.]

\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}
\label{sec:disc_groundtruth}

\looseness=-1
The near-independence between DIF flags and web-overlap labels (\Cref{sec:groundtruth}) reflects a category error: web-overlap measures global exposure while DIF measures differential functioning. Semi-synthetic dose-response confirms detection works when present; the disconnect is in the ground truth.

\subsection{Recommended DIF Audit Protocol}
\label{sec:disc_protocol}

\looseness=-1
Item-level properties do not reliably predict DIF susceptibility (AUROC $= 0.60$; \Cref{app:predictive}), confirming that post-hoc monitoring cannot be replaced by pre-screening.
We propose temporal MH-DIF whenever $\geq$500 models accumulate, with $\rho$-degradation-grounded action levels (\Cref{app:fig_rho_degradation}):
(1)~\emph{Monitor} (C\% $< 15\%$): report C\% in release notes.
(2)~\emph{Flag} ($15$--$25\%$): report stable-item rankings as sensitivity analysis alongside full rankings.
(3)~\emph{Act} (C\% $\geq 25\%$): prioritize flagged items for expert review; report stable-item rankings as primary metric; use DIF pre-screening for equating anchors~\citep{kolen2014equating,habba2026growingpains}.

